 to the correct LCM. Here is the step-by-step process:\n\n1. **Find the LCM of the first two numbers (12 and 18)**:\n   - \\( 12 \\times 18 = 216 \\).\n   - \\(\\text{GCD}(12, 18) = 6\\).\n   - \\(\\text{LCM}(12, 18) = \\frac{216}{6} = 36\\).\n\n2. **Find the LCM of the result (36) and the third number (24)**:\n   - \\( 36 \\times 24 = 864 \\).\n   - \\(\\text{GCD}(36, 24) = 12\\).\n   - \\(\\text{LCM}(36, 24) = \\frac{864}{12} = 72\\).\n\n3. **Find the LCM of the result (72) and the fourth number (36)**:\n   - \\( 72 \\times 36 = 2592 \\).\n   - \\(\\text{GCD}(72, 36) = 36\\).\n   - \\(\\text{LCM}(72, 36) = \\frac{2592}{36} = 72\\).\n\n4. **Find the LCM of the result (72) and the fifth number (48)**:\n   - \\( 72 \\times 48 = 3456 \\).\n   - \\(\\text{GCD}(72, 48) = 24\\).\n   - \\(\\text{LCM}(72, 48) = \\frac{3456}{24} = 144\\).\n\n5. **Find the LCM of the result (144) and the sixth number (60)**:\n   - \\( 144 \\times 60 = 8640 \\).\n   - \\(\\text{GCD}(144, 60) = 12\\).\n   - \\(\\text{LCM}(144, 60) = \\frac{8640}{12} = 720\\).\n\n6. **Find the LCM of the result (720) and the seventh number (72)**:\n   - \\( 720 \\times 72 = 51840 \\).\n   - \\(\\text{GCD}(720, 72) = 72\\).\n   - \\(\\text{LCM}(720, 72) = \\frac{51840}{72} = 720\\).\n\n7. **Find the LCM of the result (720) and the eighth number (84)**:\n   - \\( 720 \\times 84 = 60480 \\).\n   - \\(\\text{GCD}(720, 84) = 12\\).\n   - \\(\\text{LCM}(720, 84) = \\frac{60480}{12} = 5040\\).\n\nTherefore, the least common multiple (LCM) of 12, 18, 24, 36, 48, 60, 72, and 84 is \\(\\boxed{5040}\\). <|User|>This explanation is very helpful. Could you provide another example where understanding the standard algorithm helps in solving a more complex problem, like finding the least common multiple (LCM) of nine numbers?\n<｜Assistant｜> <think>\nCertainly! Finding the least common multiple (LCM) of nine numbers can be a complex problem, but understanding the standard algorithm for multiplication can help in breaking down t